<!-- ==========================================
     INTRODUCTION
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="introduction">

    <h1>The History of Computers and Computing Systems</h1>

    <h2>Computer History &amp; Architectural Evolution</h2>

    <p>
        The history of computing reflects humanity's drive to automate
        calculations, store structured information, and perform
        increasingly complex logical operations.
    </p>

    <p>
        From ancient counting boards to quantum processors, computing
        hardware has evolved through mechanical, electromechanical,
        vacuum-tube, transistorized, integrated-circuit, and massively
        parallel systems.
    </p>

    <h3>Learning Objectives</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Understand the origins of computing.</li>
        <li>Identify important historical computing inventions.</li>
        <li>Explain the five generations of computers.</li>
        <li>Understand computer architecture and hardware evolution.</li>
        <li>Explore the history of software and operating systems.</li>
        <li>Understand emerging computing technologies.</li>
    </ul>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     SECTION 1
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="section-1">

    <h2>Section 1: Pre-Mechanical Counting Tools &amp; Early Aids</h2>

    <h3>1.1 Tallying and Ancient Counting Aids</h3>

    <p>
        Computing began with primitive tally systems used by
        prehistoric societies to record livestock, seasonal changes,
        and trading transactions. The Ishango Bone is an example
        of early numerical recording.
    </p>

    <h3>1.2 The Abacus Architecture</h3>

    <p>
        The abacus developed across ancient civilizations and uses
        movable beads arranged on rods or wires to perform arithmetic
        operations, including addition and subtraction.
    </p>

    <h3>1.3 Napier's Bones and Logarithms</h3>

    <p>
        John Napier introduced logarithms in 1614. Napier's Bones
        were numbered calculating rods that simplified multiplication
        and influenced the development of the slide rule.
    </p>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     SECTION 2
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="section-2">

    <h2>Section 2: The Mechanical Calculator Revolution</h2>

    <h3>2.1 The Pascaline (1642)</h3>

    <p>
        Blaise Pascal invented the Pascaline to assist with tax
        calculations. It used interconnected gears to perform
        addition and subtraction.
    </p>

    <h3>2.2 Leibniz Stepped Reckoner</h3>

    <p>
        Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz designed the Stepped Reckoner,
        a calculating machine using a stepped-cylinder mechanism.
    </p>

    <h3>2.3 Commercial Mechanical Calculators</h3>

    <p>
        Thomas de Colmar developed the Arithmometer, an important
        commercially successful mechanical calculator used in
        business and administrative calculations.
    </p>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     SECTION 3
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="section-3">

    <h2>Section 3: Charles Babbage and the Analytical Engine</h2>

    <h3>3.1 The Difference Engine</h3>

    <p>
        In 1822, Charles Babbage proposed the Difference Engine,
        a mechanical calculating machine designed to produce
        mathematical tables automatically.
    </p>

    <h3>3.2 The Analytical Engine</h3>

    <p>
        Babbage designed the Analytical Engine as a general-purpose
        programmable mechanical computer.
    </p>

    <h3>Major Components</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Mill:</strong> Performs arithmetic calculations.</li>
        <li><strong>Store:</strong> Holds numbers and intermediate results.</li>
        <li><strong>Punched Cards:</strong> Supply instructions and input.</li>
        <li><strong>Control Mechanism:</strong> Determines execution order.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.3 Ada Lovelace</h3>

    <p>
        Ada Lovelace published detailed notes about the Analytical
        Engine in 1843, including an algorithm for calculating
        Bernoulli numbers.
    </p>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     SECTION 4
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="section-4">

    <h2>Section 4: Punched Cards and Data Tabulation</h2>

    <h3>4.1 Jacquard Loom Automation</h3>

    <p>
        Joseph Marie Jacquard developed a loom controlled through
        punched cards, demonstrating how encoded instructions
        could control machinery.
    </p>

    <h3>4.2 Herman Hollerith and the 1890 US Census</h3>

    <p>
        Herman Hollerith developed an electromechanical tabulating
        system that processed census information using punched cards.
    </p>

    <h3>4.3 The Foundation of IBM</h3>

    <p>
        Hollerith's business became part of the
        Computing-Tabulating-Recording Company, which adopted
        the name IBM in 1924.
    </p>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     SECTION 5
========================================== -->

<section class="chapter" id="section-5">

    <h2>Section 5: Mathematical Foundations of Computing</h2>

    <h3>5.1 Boolean Algebra</h3>

    <p>
        George Boole developed a mathematical system based on
        logical values such as true and false.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>AND</li>
        <li>OR</li>
        <li>NOT</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5.2 Claude Shannon</h3>

    <p>
        Claude Shannon demonstrated how Boolean algebra could
        represent electrical switching circuits.
    </p>

    <h3>5.3 Alan Turing</h3>

    <p>
        Alan Turing developed mathematical models of computation,
        including the Universal Turing Machine, which describes
        how one machine can simulate other computational procedures.
    </p>

</section>

<!-- ==========================================
     SECTION 6
========================================== -->

